package osv

import (
	"git.jakob-moeller.cloud/jakobmoellerdev/deps/internal/refdoc"
)

// Doc is the reference documentation of the OSV advisory lookup.
var Doc = refdoc.Section{
	Title: "Security advisories (OSV)",
	Items: []string{
		"deps sends `POST https://api.osv.dev/v1/querybatch` requests of 100 packages each, concurrently, then one `GET /v1/vulns/{id}` per advisory as soon as the batch naming it returns. An advisory a query named whose document cannot be fetched (a rate-limited or failing `/v1/vulns/{id}`) fails the lookup with an error naming it: leaving it out would report the deps it affects as clean.",
		"Ecosystems: `Go` (gomod `direct`/`indirect`; the `go` and `toolchain` directives as packages `stdlib` and `toolchain`, so a vulnerable Go release gets the smallest fixed one of its line; a 2-part `go 1.22` is queried as `1.22.0` and only fixes outside its line count), `npm` (npm deps and the deno manager's `npm:` specifiers; the locked version when package-lock.json or deno.lock has one), `crates.io` (crates.io deps of cargo, with the version Cargo.lock locks; without a lock a partial requirement such as `0.4` is queried as `0.4.0` and only fixes outside its range count), `PyPI` (`pypi` datasource, PEP 503-normalized names, the locked version when the lockfile has one), `Maven` (maven-datasource deps named `group:artifact`, with the version gradle.lockfile locks, and sbt-package deps with their Scala suffix, `cats-core_2.13`, and clojure deps of the leiningen and deps-edn managers), `RubyGems` (`rubygems` datasource: gems of Gemfiles, gems.rb and gemspecs, with the version Gemfile.lock locks, else the requirement's floor), `Packagist` (`packagist` datasource: composer packages, lower-cased, with the version composer.lock locks without its `v`, else the constraint's anchor), `Pub` (`dart` datasource: hosted pub packages, with the version pubspec.lock locks, else the constraint's lower bound), `Hex` (`hex` datasource: mix and gleam packages, with the version mix.lock or manifest.toml locks, else the requirement's anchor; organization packages are not queried), `Julia` (`julia-registry` datasource: packages of the julia manager, with the version the manifest locks, else the compat entry's lower bound), `SwiftURL` (the swift, xcodegen and mint managers' repository packages, named by their repository URL without the scheme, `github.com/apple/swift-nio`, with the version Package.resolved pins, else the requirement's), and `GitHub Actions` (github.com actions; ranges are evaluated locally, as they are for Julia and SwiftURL, which OSV does not match server-side).",
		"Terraform: OSV has no Terraform ecosystem, but providers and the CLIs are Go modules with `Go` advisories (GitHub's advisory database files them there). Providers of `registry.terraform.io` and `registry.opentofu.org` (`<namespace>/<type>`, published from `github.com/<namespace>/terraform-provider-<type>`) are queried as that module with the version `.terraform.lock.hcl` locks, else an exact constraint's; the Terraform and OpenTofu CLIs (`github-releases` `hashicorp/terraform` and `opentofu/opentofu`: `required_version`, `.terraform-version`, `.opentofu-version`, `tfe_workspace`) as `github.com/hashicorp/terraform` and `github.com/opentofu/opentofu` with an exact version. A range (`~> 5.0`, `>= 1.5`) names no installed version and is not queried. Fixed versions are bare (`1.5.7`).",
		"Distribution packages: `deb`, `apk` and `rpm` deps are queried with the package name and the version as written (OSV matches server-side) in the ecosystem of the release their registry URL names (the first of several URLs that names one): deb suites by codename, with `-security`/`-updates`/`-backports` suffixes dropped (`bookworm` → `Debian:12`, also `buster`, `bullseye`, `trixie`, `forky`; `jammy` → `Ubuntu:22.04:LTS`, also `focal`, `noble`, `oracular`, `plucky`, `questing`, `resolute`; `Ubuntu:Pro:<release>` on `esm.ubuntu.com`); apk `branch=v3.20` or a `/v3.20/` path segment → `Alpine:v3.20`, `packages.wolfi.dev` → `Wolfi`, `packages.cgr.dev` → `Chainguard`; rpm repository paths on any mirror: `/rocky/9[.x]/` → `Rocky Linux:9`, `/almalinux/9[.x]/` → `AlmaLinux:9`, `/leap/15.6/` → `openSUSE:Leap 15.6`, `/tumbleweed/` → `openSUSE:Tumbleweed`, `/azurelinux/3.0/` or `/cbl-mariner/2.0/` → `Azure Linux:3` and `Azure Linux:2`, `/openEuler-24.03-LTS-SP1/` → `openEuler:24.03-LTS-SP1`, `/mageia/distrib/9/` → `Mageia:9`, `/rhel9/` or `/ubi9/` → `Red Hat` and `/SUSE/Updates/SLE-Server/15-SP5/` (also `SLE-Module-<name>` and `SLE-Micro`) → `SUSE`, of which only the release's own streams count (`Red Hat:enterprise_linux:9:`, `SUSE:Linux Enterprise Server 15 SP5`), their ranges evaluated locally with RPM ordering. Suite aliases (`stable`, `testing`, `sid`, the default deb registry's `stable`), Alpine `edge` and other repositories are not queried: their release changes over time or OSV has no ecosystem. OSV files Debian, Ubuntu and Alpine advisories under the source package, so a binary package named differently (`libssl3` of `openssl`) finds nothing. Fixed versions are returned as published, without a `0:` epoch.",
		"Not queried, because OSV has no ecosystem for them (checked 2026-11-10): container images (OSV covers the distribution packages inside an image, such as `Debian` or `Alpine`, not the image or its tag), Terraform modules (registry, git and OCI sources) and Terragrunt engines, and Helm charts.",
		"Withdrawn advisories are skipped, and aliases are reported once.",
		"Risk data: every advisory found is annotated with its CVSS base score, computed locally from the vector the OSV record carries ([github.com/pandatix/go-cvss](https://github.com/pandatix/go-cvss), MIT): a `CVSS_V4` vector wins over `CVSS_V3`, and both map to the same ratings (`CVSS 8.3 high`). A record without a vector falls back to the v3 score of `https://api.deps.dev/v3/advisories/<GHSA id>`, and one with neither to its own qualitative severity (`database_specific.severity`, rendered as `severity high`). deps.dev is also asked for the CVE aliases the advisory itself may not list. Those CVEs give the EPSS probability that the vulnerability is exploited within 30 days (`https://api.first.org/data/v1/epss`) and whether CISA lists it in its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog (one document per run). The data is rendered with the advisory in PR bodies, `deps plan`, `deps explain` and the Actions annotations (`CVSS 9.8 critical, EPSS 94%, CISA KEV`) and never changes which update is proposed; a failing lookup only leaves the fields empty.",
	},
}
